    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/gmd-8-3867-2015",
    "abstract": "Abstract. A model for the coupled mass and energy balances of snow on the ground requires representations of absorption of solar radiation by snow, heat conduction in snow, compaction of snow, transfer of heat to snow from the air and retention and refreezing of meltwater in snow. Many such models exist, but it has proven hard to relate their relative performances to the complexity of their process representations. This paper describes the systematic development of an open-source snowpack model with two levels of representation for each of the five processes mentioned above, allowing factorial experimental designs with 32 different model configurations. The model is demonstrated using driving and evaluation data recorded over one winter at an alpine site.",
    "field": "Snowpack modeling",
    "problem": "It is difficult to systematically relate the performance of existing snowpack models to the complexity of their individual process representations, which hinders understanding of model behavior and the identification of a 'best' model structure.",
    "method": "This paper proposes a factorial snowpack model with two selectable levels of complexity for five key physical processes, allowing for systematic experiments with 32 configurations to investigate how different process representations and their combinations affect model performance.",
    "task": "The task is to develop and demonstrate a factorial snowpack model with multiple configurations to systematically evaluate how different representations of key physical processes, alone and in combination, affect simulations of snowpack mass and energy balances.",
