            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "prognostic parametrization for snow albedo",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "The five parametrizations that can be switched on or off in FSM are three prognostic equations for the albedo, density and liquid water content of snow, and two diagnostic equations for the dependence of thermal conductivity on snow density and the dependence of turbulent fluxes on atmospheric stability. ... If the prognostic parametrization for snow albedo as is switched on, decreasing albedo as snow ages and increasing albedo as fresh snow falls are parametrized by das/dt = (1/\u03c4\u03b1)(amin \u2212 as) + (Sf/Sa)(amax \u2212 as)",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": false,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "FSM 1.0",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "prognostic snow density parametrization",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "The five parametrizations that can be switched on or off in FSM are three prognostic equations for the albedo, density and liquid water content of snow, and two diagnostic equations for the dependence of thermal conductivity on snow density and the dependence of turbulent fluxes on atmospheric stability. ... Snow density is set to a constant value p0 if the prognostic density parametrization is switched off. If the parametrization is switched on, the density of fresh snow is given by parameter pf and the rate of density increase is parametrized by \u2202\u03c1/\u2202t = (1/\u03c4\u03c1)(\u03c1max \u2212 \u03c1)",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
